To find the slope of the tangent line to the graph of the function $f(x) = e^{x}\cos x$ at the point $\left(0,f(0)\right)$, we first need to compute the derivative of $f(x)$, which represents the slope of the tangent line at any point $x$ on the graph.

1. Differentiate $f(x)$ with respect to $x$:
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \frac{d}{dx}[e^{x}\cos x] \\
&= e^{x}\frac{d}{dx}[\cos x] + \cos x\frac{d}{dx}[e^{x}] \\
&= e^{x}(-\sin x) + \cos x \cdot e^{x} \\
&= e^{x}\cos x - e^{x}\sin x
\end{align*}

2. Evaluate the derivative at $x=0$ to find the slope of the tangent line at this point:
\begin{align*}
f'(0) &= e^{0}\cos 0 - e^{0}\sin 0 \\
&= 1 \cdot 1 - 1 \cdot 0 \\
&= 1 - 0 \\
&= 1
\end{align*}

Therefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point $\left(0,f(0)\right)$ is $1$. This slope corresponds to the angle of inclination $\theta$ of the tangent line, where $\tan \theta = 1$. To find $\theta$, we solve $\tan \theta = 1$:

\begin{align*}
\tan \theta &= 1 \\
\theta &= \tan^{-1}(1) \\
\theta &= \frac{\pi}{4}
\end{align*}

Hence, the angle of inclination of the tangent line at the point $\left(0,f(0)\right)$ is $\boxed{\frac{\pi}{4}}$.

